# Who gets a day off? Seurat's A Sunday on La Grande Jatte
tags: ["art history","impressionism","paris 1880s","labor rights","sunday rest","social class"]
teaches: ["art-history/frieze-composition","concepts/leisure-and-labor","vocabulary/pointillism","history/working-rights"]
thumb: "band \"Who gets a day off?\" picture \"File:Georges Seurat - A Sunday on La Grande Jatte -- 1884 - Google Art Project.jpg\" colour"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "Who is in the park, and what is each doing? Why paint leisure with such strict order?"

## Who gets a day off?
sources:
    - id: aic
      title: A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884
      authors: Art Institute of Chicago
      year: 2024
      finding: Painted 1884–86, border 1888–89; 207.5 × 308.1 cm; people of every age and social class; Seurat aimed at a modern-life equivalent of a classical Greek frieze
    - id: wiki
      title: A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Figures (woman fishing, soldiers, horn player, monkey); Seurat named the Parthenon frieze's Panathenaic procession to Gustave Kahn as his model
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Sunday_Afternoon_on_the_Island_of_La_Grande_Jatte
    - id: christophe
      title: Georges Seurat (Les Hommes d'aujourd'hui, no. 368)
      authors: Jules Christophe
      year: 1890
      finding: 'A list of the painting''s figures revised by Seurat: the boatman with his pipe, the nursemaid, the old woman under a parasol, the couple with the monkey'
    - id: sunday
      title: Loi du 12 juillet 1880 and loi du 13 juillet 1906
      authors: French Republic
      year: 1906
      finding: The 1814 Sunday-rest law was repealed in 1880; weekly rest became compulsory again in 1906

