    card title "A Sunday on La Grande Jatte"

Who gets a day off? Here is a Sunday afternoon on La Grande Jatte, an island in the Seine just outside Paris.
    draw art

Its painter, Georges Seurat, began it in 1884, aged twenty-four, and worked on it for two years.
    draw seurat

    erase seurat

It is about three metres wide, and it is full of people. So who came?
    camera on ["art@[0,0.45,0.45,0.55]"]

In front, a m (@highlight art:boatman@)an in a sleeveless vest sprawls on the grass, smoking a pipe: a boatman.

Just behind him sits a gentleman in a top hat, with a cane.
    highlight art:top_hat

    camera on ["art@[0,0.2,0.4,0.5]"]

On the left, a woman in her Sunday best fishes from the bank.
    highlight art:fishing

    camera on ["art@[0.18,0.12,0.4,0.5]"]

Further back, a nursemaid, seen from behind, sits beside an old woman under a parasol.
    highlight art:nurse

    camera on ["art@[0.3,0.15,0.2,0.3]"]

A man plays a horn by the water,
    highlight art:horn

and two soldiers walk by, straight as posts.
    highlight art:soldiers

    camera on ["art@[0.6,0.06,0.4,0.6]"]

And on the right, a fashionable couple,
    highlight art:couple

    camera on ["art@[0.6,0.55,0.4,0.45]"]

the woman with a monkey on a leash.
    highlight art:monkey

    camera reset true

    ask "Find the one person looking straight at you. Tap her." spot girl on art
        intro: One person in this crowd is looking back.
        right: There she is: the little girl in white, the only one meeting your eye.
        wrong: Look at the middle of the picture, for a small figure in white.

Boatman and gentleman, nursemaid and lady: the museum calls them people of every age and social class, sharing one patch of grass.
    highlight art:boatman art:top_hat art:nurse art:couple lift true

A free Sunday was not a given. In 1880 France had scrapped its old law of Sunday rest,
    draw law_box law_line_1

and a weekly day off became law again only in 1906.
    draw law_line_2

    erase law

So the subject is new: not a hero, not a saint, (@highlight art:boatman lift true@) but a city's crowd with an afternoon to spare.

    camera on ["art@[0.62,0.06,0.38,0.6]"]

But look how they spend it. Almost nobody moves: they stand in strict profile, or face us, frozen mid-step.
    focus art:couple strength strong rest grey

    camera reset true

    quiz "Why are they so stiff? Take a guess." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 500 buttons_at.y 400
        right: Like statues on a temple: Seurat built his crowd as a frieze.
        wrong: Think of old carvings in stone, not of the park.
        * He couldn't draw motion
        + Like statues on a temple
        * Strict park rules

Seurat told the poet Gustave Kahn he wanted modern people to move like (@highlight art:fishing art:mother art:couple lift true@) the procession on the Parthenon frieze in ancient Athens.

That is why the order is so strict: a Sunday in the park, given the calm, lasting form once kept for temples and gods.
    highlight art:crowd

Even the paint is ordered: up close, it breaks into small dots of colour, laid side by side.
    camera on ["art:girl"] zoom 3

    camera on ["art@[0.6,0.06,0.4,0.6]"]

Some read the stiffness as irony instead: Parisians posing in their Sunday best, more on show than at ease.
    highlight art:couple lift true

    camera on ["art@[0,0.3,1,0.66]"]

Either way, three centuries after Bruegel's ploughman pushed a myth into t (@highlight art:figures rest paper lift true@)he corner, there is no myth left at all: the people are the whole picture.

    camera reset true

    quiz "A painter wants a supermarket queue to feel as lasting as Seurat's park. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: Each shopper still and carefully placed: an ordinary crowd built like a frieze, to last.
        wrong: Seurat did not catch a passing moment; he built one.
        * Shoppers caught mid-stride, like a photo
        * One shopper staring out at you
        * Shoppers posing, more on show than at ease
        + Each shopper still, carefully placed

So who gets a day off? Not yet everyone. But who gets painted? By 1886, anyone in the park.
    highlight art:girl lift true

